{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Patchset 2 up on change 2600. Three corrections and one measurement worth pinning, including one to my own escalation comment above.\n\nCORRECTION TO MY ESCALATION -- the playwright lever is real but smaller than implied. I relayed it as 'dominates the cold install'. Measured properly it dominates in BYTES, not because of a browser download: the published playwright package has NO postinstall script (verified in its package.json: scripts: None), so nothing downloads browsers on this path. The saving is package download and extract only. The honest numbers:\n  full npm ci             : 15 packages, 30M node_modules\n  npm ci --omit=optional  : 11 packages, 12M node_modules\n  playwright 4.8M + playwright-core 12M = 16.8M = 56% of the tree\nOn a cold-cache runner that is still the dominant download fraction, so the lever was worth taking -- it is now an optionalDependency with --omit=optional for selections containing no browser test. But 'playwright downloads browsers' would have been wrong and is deliberately not in the commit message.\n\nWALL-CLOCK MEASUREMENT IS NOT OBTAINABLE FROM THIS HOST, which is itself worth recording so nobody retries it. Paired cold runs: --omit=optional 111.8s, full 63.4s, then --omit=optional again 420.9s. Registry variance dominates and INVERTS the comparison. Against a warm cache with --offline it is ~1s either way. Counts and bytes are the only stable evidence here.\n\nTHE VERIFIED -1 ON PATCHSET 1 WAS OURS, NOT THE PRE-EXISTING FLAKE, and the substance is worse than the message. test_predates_gate_guard_parity flagged an unguarded 'make e2e-deps' in the workflow. Because the Verified lane runs workflows from TRUSTED MAIN against an arbitrary patchset, a base predating this change has no e2e-deps rule, so the bare invocation fails the step -- this fix for a repo-wide CI failure would itself have cast red votes on everyone else's changes. Now guarded with a 'make -n e2e-deps' probe (which also keeps the guard reading the tree's own Makefile, the only thing that can answer the question) plus an ::notice:: on the skip path.\n\nLOCAL/CI SKEW THAT HID IT, worth knowing generally: that gate considers only GIT-TRACKED paths. A 16718-passing local run was green while tests/e2e/_toolchain.py was still untracked, and CI failed the instant it was committed. Re-running the gate after 'git add' is the habit that catches this class.\n\nA VACUOUS TEST WAS FOUND AND THE MECHANISM DELETED RATHER THAN PROTECTED. Removing 'if _PROVISION_ATTEMPTED: return' from the hook changed nothing observable, because pytest_collection_modifyitems fires once per session -- it was dead mechanism. The right response was deleting the flag, not writing a test to defend it. _PROVISION_ERROR alone now carries every observable behaviour and every remaining branch has a killing mutation. Eight mutations applied, all eight killed.\n\nSTRONGEST EVIDENCE IN THE CHANGE: cold state (node_modules and dist removed), CI xdist config, no pre-step -> 63 passed in 442.35s with NO worker kill. On the old code the identical cold state killed two workers at the 300s cliff (2 failed, 12 passed, 7 skipped in 306.60s). 442s of wall clock, none of it charged to any test's budget.\n\nREMINDER FOR REVIEWERS: a still-red Verified on this patchset is EXPECTED and is not evidence the CI step is wrong. The Verified lane runs workflows from trusted main, so the pre-provisioning step and the npm cache cannot take effect until this merges. Only the portable in-process half is under test on the patchset -- which is why that half carries the mutation-checked coverage."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788493347582689001,"uuid":"52b9d38d-57ed-41da-aba2-d47821a87173"}